// Rep Max questions: Religious Studies (AQA 8062MA), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"grs-001","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1a","lv":1,"q":"Which word describes the Christian belief that God is one being in three persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit?","o":["Incarnation","Trinity","Atonement","Ascension"],"a":1,"x":"The Trinity is the belief in one God in three persons. Incarnation is God becoming human, atonement is making amends, and the Ascension is Jesus returning to heaven.","w":["Incarnation is God becoming human, not God being three persons in one.","","Atonement is about Jesus' death mending the link with God, not God's nature.","The Ascension is an event, Jesus returning to heaven, not a belief about God's nature."]}
{"id":"grs-002","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1a","lv":2,"q":"Why do some people say that evil and suffering are a problem for belief in the Christian God?","o":["An all-loving, all-powerful God would be expected to stop suffering","Christians believe God cannot see what happens on Earth","The Bible never mentions evil or suffering","Christians believe God took no part in creation"],"a":0,"x":"The problem of evil asks how an all-loving, all-powerful God can allow suffering. Christians respond in different ways, for example by pointing to free will.","w":["","Christians believe God is all-knowing, so this is not the problem raised.","The Bible talks about suffering a lot, for example Job and the crucifixion.","Christians believe God created the world, so this is not the problem raised."]}
{"id":"grs-003","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1a","lv":3,"q":"Which belief about life after death is held by many Roman Catholics but rejected by most Protestant churches?","o":["Purgatory, a state of purification after death before heaven","Judgement by God after death","The resurrection of the body","Heaven as eternal life with God"],"a":0,"x":"Catholics teach that some souls are cleansed in purgatory before entering heaven. Most Protestants reject this, but both groups believe in judgement, resurrection and heaven.","w":["","Both Catholics and Protestants believe in judgement, so it doesn't divide them.","Both traditions believe in resurrection, so it doesn't divide them.","Both Catholics and Protestants believe in heaven, so it doesn't divide them."]}
{"id":"grs-004","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1b","lv":1,"q":"What do Christians mean by the Ascension?","o":["Jesus rising from the dead on the third day","Jesus returning to heaven forty days after the resurrection","Jesus being baptised by John in the River Jordan","The Holy Spirit coming to the disciples"],"a":1,"x":"Christians believe that after the resurrection Jesus was taken up into heaven in front of his disciples (Acts 1). The Spirit coming at Pentecost is a separate event.","w":["This is the resurrection, which happened before the Ascension.","","This is Jesus' baptism, at the start of his ministry.","This is Pentecost, which comes after the Ascension."]}
{"id":"grs-005","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1b","lv":2,"q":"What does the word 'atonement' mean in Christian teaching?","o":["God taking human form in Jesus","A priest announcing forgiveness in confession","Jesus' death repairing the broken relationship between God and humans","Jesus rising from the dead at Easter"],"a":2,"x":"Atonement means 'at-one-ment': Christians believe Jesus' death on the cross made up for human sin and restored people's relationship with God. God becoming human is the incarnation.","w":["This is the incarnation, not atonement.","This is absolution, which is different from atonement.","","This is the resurrection; atonement is about the effect of Jesus' death."]}
{"id":"grs-006","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1b","lv":3,"q":"Which statement best matches the Protestant (especially Lutheran) view of how people are saved?","o":["Salvation is earned only by keeping the Ten Commandments","Salvation comes only through sacraments given by a priest","Salvation is God's free gift of grace, received through faith in Christ","All people are saved whatever they believe or do"],"a":2,"x":"Many Protestants teach justification by faith: salvation is God's free gift of grace, not earned by good works. Catholics also teach that salvation comes by grace, but stress faith shown in good works and the sacraments.","w":["Works can't earn salvation for Lutherans; it is by grace through faith.","Protestants say grace is received by faith, not only through a priest's sacraments.","","This is universalism; Lutherans teach salvation is received through faith in Christ."]}
{"id":"grs-007","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2a","lv":1,"q":"Which Christian sacrament uses water to welcome a person into the Church?","o":["Eucharist","Marriage","Baptism","Anointing of the sick"],"a":2,"x":"Baptism uses water, poured or by full immersion, to show cleansing from sin and entry to the Church. The Eucharist uses bread and wine; anointing uses oil.","w":["The Eucharist uses bread and wine, not water.","Marriage uses vows and rings, not water.","","Anointing of the sick uses oil, not water."]}
{"id":"grs-008","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2a","lv":2,"q":"A church service that follows a set order of prayers and readings is called:","o":["Liturgical worship","Non-liturgical worship","Charismatic worship","Private worship"],"a":0,"x":"Liturgical worship follows a fixed pattern, as in Catholic and Anglican services. Non-liturgical worship is freer, and charismatic worship stresses the Holy Spirit.","w":["","Non-liturgical worship is freer, without a fixed script.","Charismatic worship is spontaneous and focuses on the Holy Spirit.","Private worship is done alone, not a church service with a set order."]}
{"id":"grs-009","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2a","lv":2,"q":"Many Catholics go on pilgrimage to Lourdes in France because they believe that:","o":["Jesus was crucified there","St Columba founded a monastery there in 563","the first Christian church was built there","Mary appeared to a girl called Bernadette there in 1858"],"a":3,"x":"Catholics believe Mary appeared to Bernadette Soubirous at Lourdes in 1858. Many pilgrims seek healing from its spring. Iona in Scotland is linked to St Columba.","w":["Jesus was crucified in Jerusalem, not Lourdes.","St Columba's monastery was on Iona in Scotland, not Lourdes.","Lourdes is known for Mary's appearances, not for the first church.",""]}
{"id":"grs-010","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2b","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a Christian charity that works to end poverty around the world?","o":["Greenpeace","Amnesty International","Christian Aid","Médecins Sans Frontières"],"a":2,"x":"Christian Aid is supported by many UK churches and works to tackle poverty overseas. Greenpeace, Amnesty and MSF are not Christian organisations.","w":["Greenpeace is an environmental group, not a Christian charity.","Amnesty campaigns for human rights and is not a Christian charity.","","MSF is a secular medical charity, not a Christian one."]}
{"id":"grs-011","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2b","lv":1,"q":"What is evangelism?","o":["Helping refugees to settle in a new country","Praying quietly in a church building","Sharing the Christian message in order to bring others to faith","Making peace between two groups in conflict"],"a":2,"x":"Evangelism means telling others about Jesus and inviting them to become Christians. Helping the poor or making peace are different parts of the Church's work.","w":["Helping refugees is practical care, not sharing the faith.","Quiet prayer is worship, not telling others about Christ.","","Making peace is reconciliation, not evangelism."]}
{"id":"grs-012","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2b","lv":3,"q":"Why do many Christians quote the parable of the sheep and the goats (Matthew 25) when supporting work to end poverty?","o":["Jesus says the poor will always be with us, so nothing can change","Jesus teaches that wealth is a sign of God's blessing","Jesus says charity should always be kept secret","Jesus says that helping people in need is the same as helping him"],"a":3,"x":"In Matthew 25 Jesus says that what people did for 'the least of these' they did for him. Christians use it to argue that they must feed and care for the poor.","w":["'The poor you will always have' is Matthew 26, and it isn't a reason to do nothing.","The parable isn't about wealth as blessing; it is about caring for the needy.","Secret giving is from the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 6), not this parable.",""]}
{"id":"grs-013","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1a","lv":1,"q":"What does the Muslim belief in Tawhid mean?","o":["The prophethood of Muhammad","The oneness of Allah","The Day of Judgement","The existence of angels"],"a":1,"x":"Tawhid is the belief that Allah is one, with no partners or equals. It is the most important Muslim belief and the heart of the Shahadah.","w":["Confuses Tawhid with Risalah; Tawhid is about Allah, not Muhammad's role.","","Akhirah (the afterlife) is a different belief; Tawhid is Allah's oneness.","Angels (Malaikah) are a separate article of faith, not Tawhid."]}
{"id":"grs-014","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1a","lv":2,"q":"Which statement best describes the belief in al-Qadr (predestination) held by many Muslims?","o":["Humans have no choice because every action is forced","Allah knows all that will happen, yet humans are free and accountable","Allah only learns what happens after it has happened","Allah decides who goes to heaven so actions do not matter"],"a":1,"x":"Many Muslims believe Allah's knowledge covers everything, but people still have free will and will be judged on their actions. Shi'a Muslims especially stress Allah's justice and human freedom.","w":["That is fatalism; Muslims believe people still choose and are judged on it.","","Allah's knowledge is complete, including the future; He does not learn later.","Actions matter because Allah judges them; heaven is not simply pre-picked."]}
{"id":"grs-015","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1a","lv":3,"q":"Which of these is one of the five Roots of Religion (Usul ad-Din) in Shi'a Islam?","o":["Al-Qadr, predestination","Adalat, the justice of Allah","Al-Malaikah, belief in angels","Salah, daily prayer"],"a":1,"x":"The Shi'a Roots are Tawhid, Adalat, Nubuwwah, Imamah and Ma'ad. Predestination and angels belong to the Sunni articles of faith; Salah is a practice, not a Root.","w":["Al-Qadr is a Sunni article of faith, not one of the Shi'a Roots.","","Belief in angels is a Sunni article of faith, not a Shi'a Root.","Salah is a practice (Furu ad-Din), not a Root of Religion."]}
{"id":"grs-016","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1b","lv":1,"q":"Muslims believe the Qur'an was revealed to Muhammad through which angel?","o":["Mika'il","Israfil","Azra'il","Jibril"],"a":3,"x":"Muslims believe Jibril (Gabriel) brought Allah's words to Muhammad over about 23 years. Mika'il, Israfil and Azra'il are other angels with different roles.","w":["Mika'il is linked with rain and provision, not bringing the Qur'an.","Israfil will blow the trumpet on the Last Day; he did not bring the Qur'an.","Azra'il is the angel of death, not the messenger of revelation.",""]}
{"id":"grs-017","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1b","lv":1,"q":"Which holy book do Muslims believe was given to the prophet Isa (Jesus)?","o":["Injil","Tawrat","Zabur","Sahifah"],"a":0,"x":"Muslims believe Allah sent earlier books: Tawrat to Musa, Zabur to Dawud and Injil to Isa. Muslims believe the Qur'an is Allah's final, unaltered revelation.","w":["","Tawrat was given to Musa (Moses), not Isa.","Zabur was given to Dawud (David), not Isa.","Sahifah are the scrolls of Ibrahim, not the book given to Isa."]}
{"id":"grs-018","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1b","lv":3,"q":"What do Shi'a Muslims believe about the Imams?","o":["They are elected by the Muslim community after each leader dies","They receive new revelation and write new holy books","They are chosen by Allah, starting with Ali, to guide Muslims after Muhammad","They are prophets who come after Muhammad"],"a":2,"x":"Shi'a Muslims believe Allah appointed Ali and his descendants as Imams to guide Muslims. Sunni Muslims have caliphs chosen by the community, and both groups say Muhammad was the last prophet.","w":["That describes Sunni caliphs; Shi'a believe Imams are appointed by Allah.","Imams guide and interpret, but they do not receive new revelation or books.","","Muhammad is the last prophet; Imams are leaders, not prophets."]}
{"id":"grs-019","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2a","lv":1,"q":"Why do Muslims perform wudu before Salah?","o":["To mark the end of a fast","To make themselves ritually clean before praying","To show they can afford the Hajj","To prepare halal food"],"a":1,"x":"Wudu is the washing of hands, mouth, face, arms and feet before Salah. It makes a Muslim clean in body and mind to stand before Allah.","w":["A fast is ended at iftar with food or water, not with wudu.","","Wudu has nothing to do with Hajj; it is a ritual wash before prayer.","Preparing halal food is separate; wudu is washing the body before Salah."]}
{"id":"grs-020","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2a","lv":2,"q":"Which of these is one of the Shi'a Ten Obligatory Acts but is not one of the Five Pillars?","o":["Hajj","Sawm","Zakah","Khums"],"a":3,"x":"Khums is a tax of one fifth on surplus income, paid by Shi'a Muslims. Hajj, Sawm and Zakah are among the Five Pillars and also among the Ten Obligatory Acts.","w":["Hajj is a Pillar too, so it does not set the Shi'a list apart.","Sawm is a Pillar too, so it does not set the Shi'a list apart.","Zakah is a Pillar too, so it does not set the Shi'a list apart.",""]}
{"id":"grs-021","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2a","lv":3,"q":"How does the Shahadah often differ among Shi'a Muslims compared with Sunni Muslims?","o":["Many Shi'a add a statement that Ali is the friend of Allah","They leave out the name of Muhammad","They say it only once in their lifetime","They replace it with the call to Salah"],"a":0,"x":"The Sunni Shahadah has two parts: no god but Allah, and Muhammad is his messenger. Many Shi'a add a third statement about Ali as the friend (wali) of Allah.","w":["","Shi'a still say Muhammad is Allah's messenger; they add to it, not remove.","Shahadah is said daily and in prayer, not just once in a lifetime.","The call to Salah (Adhan) is separate and does not replace the Shahadah."]}
{"id":"grs-022","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2b","lv":1,"q":"In which month do Muslims fast from dawn to sunset for Sawm?","o":["Ramadan","Muharram","Shawwal","Dhul Hijjah"],"a":0,"x":"Sawm takes place in Ramadan, the ninth month, when the Qur'an was first revealed. Shawwal begins with Id-ul-Fitr, and Hajj takes place in Dhul Hijjah.","w":["","Muharram is the first month and includes Ashura; the fast is in Ramadan.","Shawwal comes after Ramadan and begins with Id-ul-Fitr.","Dhul Hijjah is the month of Hajj and Id-ul-Adha, not Sawm."]}
{"id":"grs-023","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2b","lv":2,"q":"What do Shi'a Muslims remember on Ashura?","o":["Ibrahim's willingness to sacrifice his son","The martyrdom of Husayn at Karbala","The first revelation of the Qur'an to Muhammad","The end of the month of fasting"],"a":1,"x":"Ashura marks the death of Husayn, grandson of Muhammad, at Karbala in 680 CE. Many Sunni Muslims also fast on Ashura, remembering Allah saving Musa. Id-ul-Adha remembers Ibrahim, and Id-ul-Fitr ends Ramadan.","w":["That is Id-ul-Adha, not Ashura.","","The first revelation is remembered in Ramadan (Laylat al-Qadr), not on Ashura.","That is Id-ul-Fitr, which ends Ramadan, not Ashura."]}
{"id":"grs-024","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2b","lv":2,"q":"On Hajj, what is the name for walking seven times around the Ka'bah?","o":["Sa'i","Wuquf","Ramy","Tawaf"],"a":3,"x":"Tawaf is circling the Ka'bah seven times. Sa'i is walking between Safa and Marwah, wuquf is standing at Arafat, and ramy is throwing stones at the pillars.","w":["Sa'i is walking between Safa and Marwah, not circling the Ka'bah.","Wuquf is standing in prayer on the plain of Arafat, not circling the Ka'bah.","Ramy is throwing stones at the pillars in Mina, not circling the Ka'bah.",""]}
{"id":"grs-025","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1a","lv":1,"q":"Why does the Roman Catholic Church teach that marriage cannot normally be ended by divorce?","o":["Jesus never spoke about marriage","It is only a legal contract with no religious meaning","Divorce is allowed but only for men","Marriage is a sacrament, a lifelong covenant made before God"],"a":3,"x":"Catholics believe marriage is a sacrament that lasts for life. The Church does not recognise divorce, though it can grant an annulment, which says a valid marriage never existed.","w":["Jesus did teach on marriage, saying what God has joined together must not be separated.","Catholics see marriage as far more than a legal contract; it is a sacred sacrament.","Catholic teaching on divorce is the same for men and women; it is not one-sided.",""]}
{"id":"grs-026","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1a","lv":2,"q":"What is the usual Muslim teaching about divorce?","o":["It is forbidden in all circumstances","It is encouraged whenever a couple argue","It is allowed as a last resort but strongly discouraged","It is allowed only for men, who need no reason"],"a":2,"x":"In Islam marriage is a contract and divorce is permitted, for example by talaq or khula, but Muslims are urged to try reconciliation first. Both husbands and wives have ways to end a marriage.","w":["Divorce is permitted in Islam, so 'forbidden in all circumstances' is too strong.","Islam urges reconciliation first, so arguments alone are no reason to divorce.","","Wives can also seek divorce, for example by khula, and reasons and reconciliation matter."]}
{"id":"grs-027","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1a","lv":2,"q":"Which would a humanist be most likely to use to judge whether a sexual relationship is right?","o":["Whether a priest has blessed it","Whether it is likely to produce children","Whether it follows the Ten Commandments","Whether it is consensual and harms no one"],"a":3,"x":"Humanists base morality on reason, human welfare and consent rather than on religious rules. Many therefore accept sex outside marriage if all involved agree and nobody is harmed.","w":["Humanists don't rely on religious authority, so a priest's blessing isn't the test.","Humanists don't judge sex by whether it produces children; contraception is accepted.","The Ten Commandments are religious rules; humanists base morality on reason and welfare.",""]}
{"id":"grs-028","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1b","lv":1,"q":"In Christian teaching, which is a main purpose of the family?","o":["To bring up children to know and love God","To give fathers power over everyone else","To make sure children inherit money","To keep members apart from other communities"],"a":0,"x":"Christians see the family as the place where children are raised in love and taught the faith, and as a source of stability for society.","w":["","Christians see the family as built on love and respect, not on fathers having power.","Inheritance isn't the Christian purpose of family; raising children in faith and love is.","Christians want families to be part of the wider community and church, not cut off."]}
{"id":"grs-029","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1b","lv":2,"q":"Which Bible teaching do many Christians use to support equality between men and women?","o":["Wives should submit to their husbands (Ephesians 5)","Women should keep silent in the churches (1 Corinthians 14)","God created humans in his image, male and female (Genesis 1:27)","Adam was formed first, then Eve (1 Timothy 2)"],"a":2,"x":"Genesis 1:27 says both men and women are made in God's image. Some Christians use the other passages to argue for different roles, though others read them differently.","w":["Ephesians 5 is often used to argue for different roles, not equality.","1 Corinthians 14 is often used to limit women's roles, not to support equality.","","1 Timothy 2 is often used to argue for male leadership, not equality."]}
{"id":"grs-030","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1b","lv":3,"q":"Which statement reflects a common Muslim view of men and women?","o":["Women may not own property or earn money","Equal in worth before Allah, though many see family roles as different","Men and women must have identical roles in the family","Only men are responsible to Allah for their actions"],"a":1,"x":"Muslims teach that men and women are equal before Allah. Many see roles as complementary, with the husband providing, but Islam gives women rights to own property and to earn.","w":["Islam gives women the right to own property and earn money.","","Many Muslims see family roles as complementary, not identical.","Men and women are both individually accountable to Allah for their actions."]}
{"id":"grs-031","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2a","lv":1,"q":"Which scientific theory says the universe began about 13.8 billion years ago from a tiny, hot, dense point?","o":["Evolution by natural selection","Plate tectonics","The Big Bang theory","The Steady State theory"],"a":2,"x":"The Big Bang theory says the universe expanded from an extremely hot, dense state. Evolution explains how living things change, and Steady State is an older rival theory.","w":["Evolution explains how living things change over time, not how the universe began.","Plate tectonics is about movement of Earth's crust, not the origin of the universe.","","Steady State says the universe had no beginning, so it is a rival to the Big Bang."]}
{"id":"grs-032","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2a","lv":2,"q":"What does the Muslim idea of khalifah mean for how Muslims treat the environment?","o":["Humans own the Earth and may use it however they like","Nature does not matter because only the afterlife matters","Humans are Allah's trustees on Earth and must look after it","Only animals, not plants, must be protected"],"a":2,"x":"A khalifah is a steward or trustee. Muslims believe Allah created the world and made humans responsible for caring for it, and they will answer to Allah for how they treated it.","w":["Khalifah means trustee, not owner, so humans can't use the Earth however they like.","Muslims believe how we treat the world matters, as we answer to Allah for it.","","Khalifah covers all of creation, including plants, animals and resources."]}
{"id":"grs-033","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2a","lv":2,"q":"Christians who understand 'dominion' (Genesis 1:28) as stewardship would most likely say that humans should:","o":["Use animals and nature in any way that benefits humans","Care for creation on God's behalf and use resources responsibly","Never use animals or land for any purpose","Leave nature alone because God will look after it"],"a":1,"x":"Stewardship means looking after creation as God's trustees. Some Christians read dominion as the right to rule over nature, but many stress responsibility and care.","w":["That is the 'rule over nature' reading of dominion, not the stewardship reading.","","Stewardship allows responsible use; it doesn't ban all use of animals or land.","Stewards must actively care for creation; God has given humans that responsibility."]}
{"id":"grs-034","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2b","lv":1,"q":"What does the 'sanctity of life' mean?","o":["Life is only valuable if it is of good quality","Life is holy and is a gift from God","Life belongs to the person who lives it","Life has no purpose beyond the present"],"a":1,"x":"The sanctity of life is the religious belief that human life is sacred because God created it. It is often used to argue against abortion and euthanasia.","w":["That is a quality of life view, which values life only in some conditions.","","That is a personal autonomy view, not the religious idea that life belongs to God.","Sanctity of life says life has great value because it is holy, not that it lacks purpose."]}
{"id":"grs-035","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2b","lv":3,"q":"Which statement about Christian views on abortion is accurate?","o":["Roman Catholic: acceptable if the mother chooses it","Roman Catholic: allowed freely in the first 12 weeks","Church of England: always a sin, with no exceptions","Church of England: usually wrong, but may be a lesser evil in some cases"],"a":3,"x":"Catholics teach that life begins at conception and deliberate abortion is always wrong. The Church of England strongly opposes abortion but accepts that in strictly limited cases it may be morally preferable to any other choice.","w":["Catholics teach that deliberate abortion is wrong, whatever the mother chooses.","Catholics oppose abortion at any stage, as they believe life begins at conception.","The Church of England accepts that abortion may be a lesser evil in limited cases.",""]}
{"id":"grs-036","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2b","lv":2,"q":"Which is a typical humanist argument in support of voluntary euthanasia?","o":["People should be free to choose a dignified death to avoid unbearable suffering","God alone has the right to end life","Suffering always makes a person stronger","Life must be preserved at all costs"],"a":0,"x":"Humanists do not believe in a God who controls life and death. Many stress quality of life and personal choice, with safeguards, while religious believers often stress the sanctity of life.","w":["","This is a religious argument against euthanasia; humanists don't believe in God.","Humanists don't claim suffering is good; they stress reducing unnecessary suffering.","This is the sanctity of life view used against euthanasia, not humanist support for it."]}
{"id":"grs-037","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4a","lv":1,"q":"What do pacifists believe?","o":["War is justified only if a religious leader declares it","Violence is acceptable if there is a just cause","War and violence are always wrong and disputes should be settled peacefully","Terrorism can be justified in extreme cases"],"a":2,"x":"Pacifists reject all war and violence. Just war theory allows war in some conditions, and holy war is fought for a religious cause. Quakers are well-known Christian pacifists.","w":["Mixes up pacifism with holy war; pacifists reject war whoever declares it.","That is just war thinking; pacifists say violence is never acceptable.","","Terrorism is violence, which pacifists reject in every case."]}
{"id":"grs-038","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4a","lv":2,"q":"Which of these is one of the conditions of a just war in Christian teaching?","o":["It must be a last resort after peaceful options have failed","It must be fought to gain land and resources","It must punish the enemy as harshly as possible","It must destroy the enemy's whole population"],"a":0,"x":"Just war conditions include just cause, right authority, right intention, last resort, proportionality and a reasonable chance of success. Greed or revenge are not just causes.","w":["","Greed is not a just cause; war for land or resources is aggression.","Revenge breaks proportionality; just war limits harm to what is needed.","Wiping out a population is hugely disproportionate and targets civilians."]}
{"id":"grs-039","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4a","lv":3,"q":"Which statement describes the Muslim idea of 'lesser jihad'?","o":["The inner struggle to resist temptation and be a better Muslim","A pilgrimage to Makkah in the month of Dhul Hijjah","Armed struggle in defence of Islam or against oppression, with proper authority","Any war fought to spread Islam by force"],"a":2,"x":"Lesser jihad is armed struggle with strict conditions, such as defence of faith or fighting oppression. The inner struggle is the greater jihad. Many Muslims reject the idea that it means forcing belief.","w":["That is the greater jihad, the inner struggle, not the lesser one.","That describes Hajj, a pilgrimage, which is not jihad as struggle.","","Common myth; lesser jihad is defensive with strict rules, not forced conversion."]}
{"id":"grs-040","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4b","lv":1,"q":"What does 'nuclear deterrence' mean?","o":["Getting rid of all nuclear weapons","Using nuclear weapons first to win quickly","Agreeing not to test nuclear weapons","Having nuclear weapons to discourage other countries from attacking"],"a":3,"x":"Deterrence is the idea that fear of massive retaliation stops enemies attacking. Getting rid of nuclear weapons is called disarmament.","w":["That is disarmament, the opposite of keeping weapons to deter.","First use is aggression; deterrence relies on the threat of retaliation.","That describes a test ban treaty, not deterrence.",""]}
{"id":"grs-041","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4b","lv":2,"q":"Which Archbishop chaired South Africa's Truth and Reconciliation Commission, set up after apartheid?","o":["Oscar Romero","Justin Welby","Michael Ramsey","Desmond Tutu"],"a":3,"x":"Desmond Tutu, a Christian archbishop, chaired the commission set up in 1995. It encouraged people to tell the truth about abuses and to seek forgiveness and reconciliation.","w":["Romero was a Salvadoran archbishop killed in 1980, not linked to South Africa.","Welby became Archbishop of Canterbury in 2013, long after the commission.","Ramsey was Archbishop of Canterbury in the 1960s-70s, long before the commission.",""]}
{"id":"grs-042","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4b","lv":2,"q":"Why do many Christians say nuclear weapons could never meet the just war conditions?","o":["They would kill huge numbers of civilians, which is not proportionate","They are too cheap to be a last resort","They can only be used by a legitimate government","They are not mentioned in the Bible so are banned"],"a":0,"x":"Just war theory requires proportionality and protection of civilians. Christians who oppose nuclear weapons argue that their effects are indiscriminate and far out of proportion.","w":["","Cost has nothing to do with last resort; that means peaceful options come first.","Legitimate authority is a just war condition, so it is not a reason against.","Not being in the Bible doesn't ban them; the argument is about just war principles."]}
{"id":"grs-043","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5a","lv":1,"q":"What is a hate crime?","o":["Any crime committed in anger","A crime motivated by prejudice against a person's race, religion or similar","A crime committed by a group of people","A crime committed against a religious leader"],"a":1,"x":"A hate crime is committed because of hostility towards a person's race, religion, disability, sexual orientation or similar. Christians and Muslims believe all people are created by God, so all deserve respect.","w":["Anger alone is not enough; a hate crime needs prejudice against a group trait.","","Group crime isn't the point; a hate crime is defined by its prejudiced motive.","The victim needn't be a religious leader; any prejudice-based target counts."]}
{"id":"grs-044","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5a","lv":2,"q":"Which teaching do Christians often use to support obeying the law?","o":["Romans 13: be subject to the governing authorities","Matthew 5: blessed are the peacemakers","Genesis 1: humans are made in God's image","Luke 15: the parable of the lost sheep"],"a":0,"x":"Romans 13 says governing authorities have been set up by God, so Christians should obey them. Some Christians, such as Martin Luther King, say unjust laws may be broken.","w":["","This is about peacemaking, not obeying the law.","This is about human dignity, not obeying the authorities.","This parable is about God seeking sinners, not about obeying laws."]}
{"id":"grs-045","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5a","lv":2,"q":"A Muslim teaching on murder, often quoted from Surah 5:32, says that killing one innocent person is like:","o":["Missing one of the daily prayers","Breaking a promise to Allah","Stealing from a neighbour","Killing the whole of humanity"],"a":3,"x":"Surah 5:32 says whoever kills an innocent life is as if he had killed all people. Muslims use it to show the great value Islam places on human life.","w":["Missing prayer isn't the comparison; the verse likens murder to killing all people.","Far too mild; the verse compares murder to killing the whole of humanity.","Stealing is a lesser wrong; the verse compares murder to killing everyone.",""]}
{"id":"grs-046","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5b","lv":1,"q":"Which aim of punishment is about putting people off committing crime through fear of the consequences?","o":["Reformation","Deterrence","Retribution","Protection"],"a":1,"x":"Deterrence aims to stop offenders and others from committing crime. Reformation aims to change offenders, retribution is getting 'payback', and protection keeps society safe.","w":["Reformation aims to change the offender, not frighten people off.","","Retribution is payback for the crime, not putting others off.","Protection keeps the public safe, e.g. by imprisonment, not fear of consequences."]}
{"id":"grs-047","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5b","lv":2,"q":"Which Quaker is remembered for campaigning to improve conditions for women prisoners in the 1800s?","o":["Josephine Butler","Florence Nightingale","Mary Seacole","Elizabeth Fry"],"a":3,"x":"Elizabeth Fry, a Quaker, visited Newgate prison and worked to improve conditions for women. Her work is an example of Christians trying to reform offenders.","w":["Butler campaigned against trafficking of women, not prison conditions, and wasn't a Quaker.","Nightingale was a nurse, not a prison reformer.","Seacole nursed soldiers in the Crimean War; she was not a prison reformer.",""]}
{"id":"grs-048","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5b","lv":3,"q":"What is the Roman Catholic Church's current teaching on the death penalty?","o":["It is inadmissible because it attacks the dignity of the person","It is required for all murder","It is acceptable whenever the state decides","It is allowed only for theft"],"a":0,"x":"In 2018 Pope Francis changed the Catechism to say the death penalty is inadmissible. Some other Christians and Muslims accept it for the most serious crimes.","w":["","Catholic teaching does not require execution; it now opposes it.","The Church doesn't leave this to the state; it calls the death penalty inadmissible.","The Church now rejects the death penalty for every crime, theft included."]}
